\chapter{Asynchronous I/O and Capability Transports}
\label{ch:io}

High-performance microkernel I/O architectures face a persistent
dichotomy. Traditional synchronous IPC provides elegant network
transparency but incurs severe overhead during bulk data transfer.
Conversely, modern shared-memory ring buffers (such as
\texttt{io\_uring}) provide massive bulk throughput by amortizing
syscall overhead, but introduce unnecessary latency and memory
contention when used for intra-kernel communication where no privilege
boundary exists.

Telix resolves this via a unified, **purely asynchronous I/O model**. By
fusing traditional capability ports with dynamically bound lock-free
memory rings, Telix provides a single unified notification subsystem for
network, IPC, and storage events, ensuring cluster-wide extensibility
without sacrificing local bare-metal throughput.

\section{The Asynchronous Driver Burden and Unified Purity}
\label{sec:io_purity}

A foundational design constraint of the Telix Framekernel is that
execution threads must never block on I/O. This "purity" of asynchronous
design is highly consequential: it enables the M:N scheduler to pack
thousands of I/O-bound tasks onto a handful of physical CPU cores
without exhausting kernel stack memory.

Consequently, all peripheral drivers, filesystem layers, and network
stacks are implemented as stackless Rust \texttt{Future} state machines.
\begin{description}
    \item[\textbf{The Programming Burden:}] Writing drivers as continuations rather than traditional sequential threads imposes a steeper cognitive burden. Driver logic must be explicitly broken into state transitions yielding to the executor while waiting for hardware DMA or network packets.
    \item[\textbf{Unified Notification:}] The strict async requirement yields a massive architectural benefit: a single unified event notification mechanism. A continuation suspended on a network socket, a filesystem read, or a cluster-routed IPC message is awakened through the exact same Port Set capability queue.
\end{description}

\section{Bimodal Transport: Ports, Port Sets, and Rings}
\label{sec:bimodal_transport}

To maintain the network transparency of distributed capability routing
while supporting zero-copy bulk data, Telix capability channels operate
bimodally. The **Port** is the fundamental routing primitive,
addressable across local NUMA nodes and remote cluster networks alike.

\emph{Bimodal} names the two transport \emph{mechanisms} (Immediate and
Ring-Bound) described below.  These are orthogonal to the four IPC
\emph{scopes} of the matryoshka (malyshka, doch, mama, babushka) given
in Table~\ref{tab:ipc_scopes}: any scope may be served by either
mechanism, according to whether its traffic is small control messages or
bulk data.

\subsection{Immediate Mode (Fast-Path IPC)}
For control-plane operations, small IPC messages, and intra-domain
kernel interactions, traversing a ring buffer is inefficient. In
Immediate Mode, sending a message to a Port transfers a small payload
(typically capability tokens and minimal metadata) directly via CPU
registers. If the target continuation is suspended, it is immediately
migrated to the runnable queue, achieving L4-style IPC latencies.

\subsection{Ring-Bound Mode (Bulk I/O)}
For high-bandwidth dataplane operations (e.g., NVMe block storage or
100GbE packet processing), a Capability Port is bound to a shared-memory
Submission/Completion Queue (SQ/CQ). The Port itself ceases to carry
payload data; instead, it acts solely as the notification "doorbell."

\subsection{Port Sets as the Event Multiplexer}
A **Port Set** allows a driver or user-space process to aggregate
multiple Ports into a single waitable capability. When a continuation
polls a Port Set, it can simultaneously receive fast-path cluster IPC
messages and local SQ/CQ doorbell notifications, unifying distributed
and local I/O seamlessly.

\section{Lock-Free Ring Buffer Mechanics (SQ/CQ)}
\label{sec:ring_mechanics}

When a Port is operating in Ring-Bound mode, bulk asynchronous requests
communicate through a dual-ring memory structure mapped directly between
the client domain and the driver \footcite{axboe19iouring}.

To avoid cache-line bouncing, the queues are strictly divided into
single-producer, single-consumer (SPSC) arrays:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Submission Queue (SQ):} The client writes I/O descriptors into the ring and advances \texttt{sq\_tail} (publishing via a C11 \texttt{Release} barrier). The driver reads from \texttt{sq\_head}.
    \item \textbf{Completion Queue (CQ):} Upon concluding an operation (or processing a hardware interrupt), the driver writes a completion result and advances \texttt{cq\_tail}.
\end{itemize}
Because index synchronization relies on explicit atomic memory order
barriers rather than mutexes, both producer and consumer can operate
fully lock-free.

\section{Zero-Copy Payloads via Morsel Tokens}
\label{sec:zerocopy_morsels}

Passing large payloads across isolation boundaries typically requires
costly memory copies. Telix avoids this by passing **Capability Morsel
Tokens** inside the SQ/CQ descriptors rather than raw virtual addresses.

A Capability Morsel Token wraps a physical morsel --- a contiguous,
power-of-two-aligned extent from the coremapless allocator
(\S\ref{sec:mem_coremapless}) --- with access rights (read, write,
transfer).  The token is what gets passed over a Port; the underlying
physical morsel is what the IOMMU maps.

An SQ/CQ entry encodes a capability offset and length descriptor bound
to a pre-shared physical memory region. The driver domain verifies that
the request bounds fall strictly within the associated Capability Token.
This allows the driver to process the payload directly within the shared
physical frame without gaining arbitrary access to the client's broader
address space.

\section{DMA Safety Boundaries and IOMMU Isolation}
\label{sec:dma_safety}

Direct Memory Access (DMA) devices pose a severe threat to microkernel
capability boundaries. A compromised PCIe device can issue DMA writes to
arbitrary physical addresses, bypassing OS software protections.

Telix enforces **Capability-Bound DMA Isolation** by integrating memory
morsel capabilities with hardware IOMMUs (Intel VT-d, ARM SMMU, RISC-V
IOPMP) \footcite{willmann06iommu}.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Pinned Physical Pools:} Memory allocated for DMA is designated as a pinned I/O Morsel. The Framekernel locks these physical frames and inserts mappings into the hardware IOMMU table specific to that device's PCIe domain.
    \item \textbf{Dynamic vs. Static Bindings:} For absolute maximum throughput (e.g., 100GbE NICs), morsels are statically pre-mapped into the IOMMU. For higher security environments, the IOMMU table is dynamically populated immediately prior to the DMA transaction and flushed upon CQ completion, mitigating stale DMA window exploitation.
\end{itemize}

\section{Beyond Block I/O: Filesystems and Storage}
\label{sec:io_filesystems}

While the mechanisms described in this chapter govern the efficient
transport of raw blocks, packets, and IPC messages, they do not dictate
the structural semantics of hierarchical storage.

Translating raw block I/O into a distributed, capability-indexed
namespace requires conflict-free replicated data types (CRDTs),
lease-based mutation, and robust distributed caching logic. The
architecture of the Telix Distributed Filesystem and its integration
with the unified Port Set notification model is detailed extensively in
Chapter~\ref{ch:filesystem}.
